\begin{document}

\section{Motivation}
Over the last 10.000 years, humans have reshaped large portions of the earths surface by cutting down trees and replaced them with crops, substituting forests with farmlands. In 2013 a database of forest coverage and deforestation for the entire earth during during the period 2000-2013 was published \citet{Hansen2013}, and it has been updated regurlarly, the latest version is from 2024. The data points in this database represent very small rectangles, approximately $30\times30 \textrm{m}^2$. Figure \vref{fig:Hansen} is a visualisation of that database. In short, the motivation of this paper and the accompanying data products is to provide forest change data in a format that is easily usable for social scientists.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{hansen.png}
    \caption{Visualisation of the Global Forest Change database. Source \citet{Hansen2013}. Green represent stable forests, red is areas with deforestation, blue is new forest and pink is a mix of deforestation followed by new forest growth.}
    \label{fig:Hansen}
\end{figure}


If you want to explain why deforestation takes place, or the social consequences of deforestation, you need to put the deforestation data in context, and for this purpose you need data aggregated to some socially relevant area unit. A natural choice for such areas are the administrative areas defined by each country. Aggregating time series data for 152 billion tiny rectangles sized 30 by 30 meters to time series data for \num{34325} administrative areas is an essential step towards analyses of causes and consequences of deforestation, but it also a computational and technical challenge. This document both serves as the canonical source for how it was done, and as a manual for how to use the aggregated database.





% \section{Aim}
% The aim of this article is to document a dataproduct so that it can be used correctly. The data product contains aggregated time-series data on forest coverage and deforestation in between year 2000 and 2023 in 18956 administrative areas in Latin America, Africa and South Asia. The database can be also reconstructed from scratch by running the source code of this document (ref).

In this database, and in this document at large, \emph{forest coverage} has a precise definition: the proportion of the area that is covered by organic structures (trees) that reflects sunlight at least 5 meters above the ground. Deforestation is defined as the decrease in forestation. If an 70 per cent of an area was covered forest at time t0, and at time t1 65 per cent of the area is covered by forest, the deforestation of that area between time t0 and t1 was 5. The recorded deforestation statistic is this \emph{difference in forestation}. If you want to analyse the \emph{relative} deforestation, e.g. by calculating how large percentage of the existing forest at time t0 was lost at time t1, you have to first derive the forestation state at t1 and then take the ratio between forestation at t0 and t1.


